./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:882:The hidden-terminal construction gives a clean finite-horizon minimax lower bound: if two price processes share a long zero prefix and only differ in a terminal price distribution, then no learner can tune quotes for both before the reveal. This proves an \(\Omega(D)\) lower bound over horizon \(T=D+1\) for a broad unknown-process class.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:884:It does **not** by itself prove a process-wise necessity theorem for the raw tail condition \(\beta(h)\to 0\). The main obstruction is quantifiers: the construction is strongest when the hard process may depend on the horizon or when regret is minimax over a class of possible laws unknown to the learner.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:888:A technically cleaner version avoids the strict-threshold “max not attained” issue by replacing deterministic terminal prices with small continuous terminal intervals.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:908:V_L:=\max_a g_L(a)=1/2,
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:910:V_H:=\max_a g_H(a)=3/4,
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:916:\max_a \frac{g_L(a)+g_H(a)}2 = 1/2.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:919:Therefore under the uniform prior over the two worlds, every learner has Bayes regret at least
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:922:D\left(\frac{V_L+V_H}{2}-\max_a\frac{g_L(a)+g_H(a)}2\right)
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:929:By Yao/minimax, for every randomized learner, at least one of the two worlds gives regret \(\ge D/8\).
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:940:This is a finite-horizon minimax lower bound over the two terminal-distribution worlds above.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:955:so this construction supports a lower-bound term of order \(T\beta(H)\) when the delayed tail corresponds to genuinely valuable, indistinguishable rewards.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:988:So \(g_L\) is maximized at \(a=1/2\), \(g_H\) at \(a=3/4\). A case check gives
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:991:\max_a \frac{g_L(a)+g_H(a)}2=1/2.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1000:If the learner is allowed to know the exact law, the lower bound disappears: in world \(L\), quote \(1/2\); in world \(H\), quote \(3/4\).
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1002:Naive block-splicing with fresh random terminal types does not work for the stated regret definition, because the comparator is \(\max_a \mathbb E[\sum_t r_t(a)]\), not \(\mathbb E[\max_a \sum_t r_t(a)]\). With iid hidden block types, the best fixed comparator may be exactly the same Bayes action available to the learner.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1004:A fixed infinite deterministic block construction might work only under an adversarial or learner-dependent sequence, or along horizons dominated by a final super-long block. That would need separate quantifier work.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1008:The raw condition \(\sup_t\sup_a\operatorname{ess\,sup}\Pr(h<\tau_t(a)<\infty\mid P_{\le t})\to0\) is probably too strong as a process-wise necessity condition.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1010:Example obstruction: arbitrarily long delayed hits at tiny prices \(p_m\downarrow0\) can keep \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\), while the total attainable comparator value may still be \(o(T)\). Then sublinear regret can be trivial despite failing the raw tail condition.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1012:Another obstruction: if every long-delayed terminal threshold is known from the process law, long delays alone do not force regret. The lower bound needs **indistinguishability between separated valuable optima**, not merely delayed hitting.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1032:Formalize the single-block lemma as a minimax theorem with explicit quantifiers over learner knowledge and horizon-dependent process classes.
./iterations/iter_001/promising_ideas.json:1:{"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"This may only repackage the upper bound and not give necessity. It may also require horizon-aware choices of H and K or prior knowledge of the tail profile unless a doubling/adaptive scheme is added.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","quantifier audit","rate optimization"],"summary":"Turn the solver's Delta_T(H) truncation into a precise theorem for a process class C: for any H,K there is a grid delayed-full-information learner with sup_{P in C} R_T(P) <= C sqrt(T H log K)+T/K+sup_{P in C} Delta_T^P(H), where Delta_T^P(H)=sup_a sum_t E[r_t(a)-r_t^H(a)]. Then derive exact qualitative condition: existence of H_T,K_T with H_T log K_T=o(T), T/K_T=o(T), and uniform Delta_T(H_T)=o(T).","title":"Uniform Delta Tail Upper Bound","why_promising":"It is the cleanest rigorous sufficient condition already latent in the solver attempt, strictly weaker than the beta essential-sup condition, and gives immediate rate formulas once Delta_T is estimated."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"This will prove a minimax obstruction for broad unknown classes, not a process-wise necessity theorem. Care is needed if the problem forbids horizon-dependent processes or assumes a fixed stochastic law.","suggested_lenses":["lower bound","Yao principle","counterexample search"],"summary":"Formalize a finite-horizon indistinguishability lower bound. Use two processes with identical prefix P_1=...=P_D=0 and different terminal reveal P_{D+1}=p0 or p1, e.g. p0=1/2 and p1=1. Before the reveal, any learner must use the same quote distribution under both worlds, but the best fixed quote is near p0 in one world and near p1 in the other, yielding constant regret per early round and Omega(D) total regret. Then explore block-splicing to obtain a fixed infinite process or class-level lower bound.","title":"Hidden Terminal Price Lower Bound","why_promising":"It directly captures why long unobserved hitting delays can force linear regret in distribution-free settings and gives a crisp target for critics to verify."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The right sigma-field and norm may become too abstract to yield a usable theorem. Known-law predictability can make every process trivial, so the condition must be tied to what the learner is allowed to know or estimate.","suggested_lenses":["characterization","martingale decomposition","statistical decision theory"],"summary":"Replace raw long-delay mass by unpredictable long-delay value. Decompose z_t^H(a)=r_t(a)-r_t^H(a) into a predictable component, such as E[z_t^H(a)|available short-horizon information], and an innovation/residual component. Seek an upper bound depending on learnable predictable surrogates plus the residual tail, and a lower bound from pairs of laws with small observable-distance but separated predictable tail values.","title":"Predictability-Aware Tail Condition","why_promising":"It explains both sides of the solver attempt: iid-uniform has large raw beta but predictable expected rewards, while hidden-block examples have genuinely unpredictable delayed value."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"This addresses stochastic i.i.d. or mixing subclasses rather than adversarial adaptive processes. Endpoint behavior and atoms can make the optimal quote move with T and complicate uniform convergence.","suggested_lenses":["positive example","empirical process","rate derivation"],"summary":"Study the unknown i.i.d. price class as a concrete countermodel to beta-necessity. Since prices are observed immediately, estimate F from P_1,...,P_t and optimize G_T(a)=sum_{L=0}^{T-1} a(1-F(a)^L). Prove sublinear regret from DKW/threshold uniform convergence, including cases like continuous support up to 1 where beta(h)=1 for all h.","title":"Unknown IID Prices Despite Beta Failure","why_promising":"It could produce a strong theorem showing that even law-unaware learners can beat the beta condition in natural stochastic classes, not just in the solver's law-aware example."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Near-optimal sets are horizon-dependent and may be unknown to the learner. If bad long-tail actions are needed for exploration, a near-optimal-only condition may be insufficient.","suggested_lenses":["refined sufficient condition","localization","counterexample search"],"summary":"Investigate localized tail conditions over relevant actions rather than all a. Define a near-optimal set A_T(epsilon)={a: V_T(a) >= sup_b V_T(b)-epsilon T} and a localized truncation Delta_T^{loc}(H,epsilon)=sup_{a in A_T(epsilon)} sum_t E[r_t(a)-r_t^H(a)]. Try to combine localization/experts-with-elimination with truncation so the algorithm pays long-delay cost only for plausible comparator actions.","title":"Localized Tail Around Good Quotes","why_promising":"The beta supremum is often driven by pathological quotes near the top of the support. Regret should only care about actions that can compete with the best fixed quote."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"General delayed-experts lower bounds may not embed into the market-making threshold reward structure. The threshold class may have lower complexity and better rates than arbitrary K experts.","suggested_lenses":["rate lower bound","embedding","sequential complexity"],"summary":"Seek matching rates for the upper bound. For bounded delay H, prove or refute an Omega(sqrt(T H)) lower bound within the market-making model, not just generic delayed experts. Try to encode hard threshold decisions through carefully chosen future price maxima M_{t,H}=max_{s<=H} P_{t+s}, so r_t^H(a)=a 1{a<M_{t,H}} realizes a rich enough online threshold class.","title":"Matching Delay-Rate Lower Bounds","why_promising":"The current upper rate sqrt(T H log K)+T/K may be loose because market-making rewards are one-dimensional thresholds. Matching or improving it is needed for a full rate theorem."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"This is foundational rather than a final characterization. It may conclude that the original question is ill-posed unless the model class and learner knowledge are fixed.","suggested_lenses":["definition audit","theorem statement","obstruction"],"summary":"Write a quantifier taxonomy. Prove that for a known process law, regret to the best fixed quote is trivial: play an exact or epsilon-optimal fixed quote for the horizon, giving zero or O(1) regret. Separate this from universal guarantees over a class, minimax guarantees, individual-sequence regret, horizon-dependent adversaries, and adaptive stochastic processes.","title":"Quantifier Taxonomy And Triviality","why_promising":"A necessary-and-sufficient condition cannot be correct until 'possible' is pinned down. This direction prevents proving a false necessity statement and guides all lower-bound claims."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"Algorithmic exactness may distract from the statistical characterization. Continuous-action algorithms may still need discretization near atoms or strict inequalities.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","threshold geometry","upper-bound improvement"],"summary":"Exploit the continuum threshold structure directly. For truncated horizon H, after H steps the learner observes r_t^H(a)=a 1{a<M_{t,H}} for all a, a piecewise-linear/threshold reward curve. Investigate whether exponential weights or FTRL can be implemented over intervals without a K-grid and whether regret can be bounded by threshold-class complexity rather than log K plus T/K discretization.","title":"Continuum Threshold Learner","why_promising":"It may sharpen upper rates and remove discretization artifacts, making any eventual characterization cleaner and less tied to arbitrary grids."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:4:Attempt: Prove that sublinear regret is possible only if the essential-sup hitting tail \(\beta(h)\to0\).
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:5:Failure: False under law-aware/process-wise quantifiers. For iid uniform prices, \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\), but a learner knowing the law can play the best fixed quote in expectation and get zero regret.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:11:Failure: Singleton terminal-spike law has zero law-aware regret but \(\Delta_T(H)=\Omega(T)\) for \(H=o(T)\).
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:16:Attempt: View \((r_t^H(b))_{b\in\mathcal G_K}\) as a delayed full-information reward vector in the original market protocol.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:23:Failure: Regret compares to \(\sup_a\mathbb E\sum_t r_t(a)\). With iid block types, the best fixed expected comparator can be the same Bayes action available to the learner, so per-block realized optimal-action gaps do not add.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:29:Failure: For fixed \(D\), delay is bounded by \(D\), so asymptotic regret is \(O(D)=o(T)\) and \(\beta(h)=0\) for \(h\ge D\).
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:33:## Using `max_a` without checking attainment
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:34:Attempt: State regret and optimal quotes with \(\max_{a\in[0,1]}\).
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:36:Lesson: Use \(\sup_a\) throughout, or impose continuity/no-atom/attainment conditions.
./working_notes/promising_directions.md:6:\delta_T^P(H)=\sup_a\mathbb E\sum_t r_t(a)-\sup_a\mathbb E\sum_t r_t^H(a).
./working_notes/promising_directions.md:9:Critical checks: Use \(\sup_a\), horizon-indexed classes \(\mathcal C_T\), one uniform schedule \(H_T,K_T\), and a precise delayed-experts lemma.
./working_notes/promising_directions.md:11:## 2. Formalize the finite-horizon hidden-terminal minimax lower bound
./working_notes/promising_directions.md:14:Critical checks: State learner knowledge; apply the uniform two-world prior carefully; avoid claiming fixed-law necessity.
./working_notes/promising_directions.md:19:Possible form: A class contains two laws with identical observable prefix for \(D\) rounds and comparator curves separated by constant Bayes gap.
./working_notes/promising_directions.md:20:Falsifier: Known-law singleton examples with large delayed mass but zero regret.
./working_notes/promising_directions.md:22:## 4. Build strict-separation counterexamples
./working_notes/promising_directions.md:23:Goal: Formalize examples where \(\beta\) fails but average truncation succeeds, and where both \(\beta\) and \(\Delta\) fail but regret is still zero due to known structure.
./working_notes/promising_directions.md:25:Candidates: Rare bad histories for essential-sup failure; tiny-price long-delay blocks for value-free delay; terminal-spike singleton for predictable delay.
./working_notes/promising_directions.md:32:## 6. Adaptivity to unknown tail profiles
