./logs/iter_001_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:5:The biggest break is quantifier/model-level: if future prices can depend on learner actions, then the delayed full-information reduction is not justified. The second biggest issue is that the proposed \(\Delta_T(H)\) is stronger than needed and is mislabeled as “best comparator” mass, even though it is defined as a supremum over all actions.
./logs/iter_001_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:10:   The reduction treats \((r_t^H(b))_{b\in\mathcal G_K}\) as delayed full-information reward vectors. This is valid if the price path is exogenous or oblivious to learner actions. If \(P_{t+s}\) may depend on actions chosen after time \(t\), then the reward vector for round \(t\) is generated partly by future learner behavior, outside the standard delayed-experts model.
./logs/iter_001_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:12:2. **Missing assumption: counterfactual meaning of regret.**  
./logs/iter_001_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:18:   \Delta_T^P(H)=\sup_a \sum_t \mathbb E[r_t(a)-r_t^H(a)],
