./logs/iter_001_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:26:   \sup_a \mathbb E\sum_t r_t^H(a),
./logs/iter_001_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:36:6. **Technical gap: max versus sup.**  
./logs/iter_001_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:37:   Because rewards use the strict event \(P>a\), the best action need not be attained. The theorem should use \(\sup_a\), or add compactness/continuity assumptions ensuring a maximizer.
./logs/iter_001_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:39:7. **Unsupported strength claim: “strictly weaker than \(\beta\).”**  
./logs/iter_001_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:53:Then every \(t<T\) has expected reward \(a(1-a)\), maximized at \(a=1/2\). A learner knowing this singleton law gets zero regret by always quoting \(1/2\). But for \(H=o(T)\),
./logs/iter_001_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:59:Stress test this further with a fixed infinite process: deterministic spike times with growing gaps, where spike prices are i.i.d. uniform. The known optimal quote remains \(1/2\), while \(\beta(h)\) can fail badly along long gaps.
