./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:455:Formalize a finite-horizon indistinguishability lower bound. Use two processes with identical prefix P_1=...=P_D=0 and different terminal reveal P_{D+1}=p0 or p1, e.g. p0=1/2 and p1=1. Before the reveal, any learner must use the same quote distribution under both worlds, but the best fixed quote is near p0 in one world and near p1 in the other, yielding constant regret per early round and Omega(D) total regret. Then explore block-splicing to obtain a fixed infinite process or class-level lower bound.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:458:It directly captures why long unobserved hitting delays can force linear regret in distribution-free settings and gives a crisp target for critics to verify.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:461:This will prove a minimax obstruction for broad unknown classes, not a process-wise necessity theorem. Care is needed if the problem forbids horizon-dependent processes or assumes a fixed stochastic law.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:477:The hidden-terminal construction gives a clean finite-horizon minimax lower bound: if two price processes share a long zero prefix and only differ in a terminal price distribution, then no learner can tune quotes for both before the reveal. This proves an \(\Omega(D)\) lower bound over horizon \(T=D+1\) for a broad unknown-process class.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:479:It does **not** by itself prove a process-wise necessity theorem for the raw tail condition \(\beta(h)\to 0\). The main obstruction is quantifiers: the construction is strongest when the hard process may depend on the horizon or when regret is minimax over a class of possible laws unknown to the learner.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:483:A technically cleaner version avoids the strict-threshold “max not attained” issue by replacing deterministic terminal prices with small continuous terminal intervals.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:503:V_L:=\max_a g_L(a)=1/2,
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:505:V_H:=\max_a g_H(a)=3/4,
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:511:\max_a \frac{g_L(a)+g_H(a)}2 = 1/2.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:514:Therefore under the uniform prior over the two worlds, every learner has Bayes regret at least
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:517:D\left(\frac{V_L+V_H}{2}-\max_a\frac{g_L(a)+g_H(a)}2\right)
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:524:By Yao/minimax, for every randomized learner, at least one of the two worlds gives regret \(\ge D/8\).
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:535:This is a finite-horizon minimax lower bound over the two terminal-distribution worlds above.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:550:so this construction supports a lower-bound term of order \(T\beta(H)\) when the delayed tail corresponds to genuinely valuable, indistinguishable rewards.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:583:So \(g_L\) is maximized at \(a=1/2\), \(g_H\) at \(a=3/4\). A case check gives
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:586:\max_a \frac{g_L(a)+g_H(a)}2=1/2.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:595:If the learner is allowed to know the exact law, the lower bound disappears: in world \(L\), quote \(1/2\); in world \(H\), quote \(3/4\).
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:597:Naive block-splicing with fresh random terminal types does not work for the stated regret definition, because the comparator is \(\max_a \mathbb E[\sum_t r_t(a)]\), not \(\mathbb E[\max_a \sum_t r_t(a)]\). With iid hidden block types, the best fixed comparator may be exactly the same Bayes action available to the learner.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:599:A fixed infinite deterministic block construction might work only under an adversarial or learner-dependent sequence, or along horizons dominated by a final super-long block. That would need separate quantifier work.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:603:The raw condition \(\sup_t\sup_a\operatorname{ess\,sup}\Pr(h<\tau_t(a)<\infty\mid P_{\le t})\to0\) is probably too strong as a process-wise necessity condition.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:605:Example obstruction: arbitrarily long delayed hits at tiny prices \(p_m\downarrow0\) can keep \(\beta(h)=1\) for all \(h\), while the total attainable comparator value may still be \(o(T)\). Then sublinear regret can be trivial despite failing the raw tail condition.
./logs/iter_001_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:607:Another obstruction: if every long-delayed terminal threshold is known from the process law, long delays alone do not force regret. The lower bound needs **indistinguishability between separated valuable optima**, not merely delayed hitting.
