./logs/iter_001_synthesis.stdout.log:2:- The essential-sup tail condition \(\beta(h)\to0\) is accepted as a sufficient condition under exogenous/oblivious prices, via truncation, grid discretization, and delayed full-information experts.
./logs/iter_001_synthesis.stdout.log:4:- The hidden-terminal two-world construction gives a valid finite-horizon minimax lower bound over an unknown class: regret \(\Omega(D)\) at horizon \(T=D+1\).
./logs/iter_001_synthesis.stdout.log:7:- Formalize the upper theorem with \(\delta_T(H)\), \(\sup_a\), horizon-indexed classes, and a cited/proved delayed-experts lemma.
./logs/iter_001_synthesis.stdout.log:12:- The original “\(\beta(h)\to0\) iff sublinear regret” conjecture is refuted under law-aware fixed-process quantifiers by iid uniform prices.
./logs/iter_001_synthesis.stdout.log:13:- \(\Delta_T(H)=o(T)\) is not necessary: the known terminal-spike singleton has zero regret but linear truncation loss.
