./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:625:Facts: For each early quote, expected reward is \(a(1-a)\), maximized at \(a=1/2\). The learner can quote \(1/2\) and get zero regret. For any \(H=o(T)\), most early rewards are excluded by truncation, so \(\Delta_T(H)\approx (T-H)/4=\Omega(T)\).
./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:626:Why it matters: Large truncation loss can be harmless when the delayed reward curve is known/predictable.
