./logs/iter_002_ideas.stderr.log:755:{"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"Cannot get sharp necessity; upper theorem may just restate existing sufficient route if delayed-experts citation remains external.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","rate optimization"],"summary":"Prove and sharpen the exogenous-process theorem using the comparator-gap truncation loss delta_T(H), including exact horizon-indexed quantifiers, sup over quotes, grid rounding, and a self-contained delayed-experts bound or precise citation. Ask whether rates can be expressed as inf_{H,K}{sqrt(T H log K)+T/K+Delta(H)} or with delta, and optimize for polynomial/log tails.","title":"Finalize comparator-gap upper theorem","why_promising":"This is the most solid accepted path and gives a theorem that is true even after beta-necessity and Delta-necessity failed."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"May only yield finite-horizon or horizon-dependent class lower bounds, not a fixed-process characterization.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","lower bound"],"summary":"Formalize the hidden-terminal two-world lower bound and generalize it into a reusable lemma: if two laws have identical observable prefixes for D rounds but their expected delayed reward curves have Bayes gap gamma per early round, then any uniform learner over the class has regret at least gamma D in one world. Explore q-scaled reveal probability and multiple separated intervals.","title":"General hidden-terminal lower-bound lemma","why_promising":"It isolates the real obstruction as valuable, indistinguishable delayed reward rather than raw delay probability."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The proposed condition may become a lower-bound template rather than a necessary-and-sufficient characterization; predictability is hard to encode cleanly.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","counterexample search"],"summary":"Develop a refined minimax characterization in terms of predictable versus unpredictable truncation loss. For a class C_T, define an information-theoretic tail obstruction as the maximal Bayes gap caused by rewards arriving after H among laws indistinguishable up to H, and compare it to the sufficient delta_T(H). Seek a theorem of the form sublinear regret iff there are H_T=o(T/log T) for which both short-delay learning cost and this indistinguishable-tail modulus are o(T).","title":"Information modulus for delayed value","why_promising":"Known singleton counterexamples show raw beta and Delta are not necessary because the learner can know or predict the delayed reward curve; this direction adds the missing ignorance component."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Constructions may be technically messy around strict inequality P>a, quote a=0, and all-horizon asymptotics.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","toy model"],"summary":"Produce explicit strict-separation examples: (a) rare bad histories make essential-sup beta fail while average delta or Delta is o(T); (b) deterministic long zero blocks followed by prices p_m decreasing fast enough give beta(h)=1 along arbitrarily large h but total comparator value o(T); (c) terminal-spike singleton keeps Delta_T(H)=Omega(T) but law-aware regret zero. Compute delta, Delta, beta, and optimal value for each.","title":"Strict counterexample suite","why_promising":"These examples will pin down exactly which necessity claims are false and force any final characterization to include value and information."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"A single fixed process may still wash out per-block gaps under expectation against one fixed comparator, as the iid block-splicing attempt did.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","lower bound"],"summary":"Revisit asymptotic fixed-process lower bounds using deterministic super-growing blocks rather than iid blocks. Make horizons T_n end just before a block's terminal reveal, with block n dominating all previous blocks, and choose block type or terminal distribution so the best fixed quote for horizon T_n differs from what is learnable before reveal. Check carefully that regret is against sup_a E sum_t r_t(a), not E sup_a.","title":"Super-growing block obstruction","why_promising":"If it works, it would bridge the gap between finite-horizon minimax lower bounds and a genuine fixed-law or horizon-wise asymptotic obstruction."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"May conclude that no meaningful theorem is possible without stronger protocol restrictions; action-dependent counterfactuals can be underdefined.","suggested_lenses":["model audit","formalization"],"summary":"Audit the model variants: exogenous stochastic process, oblivious adversarial sequence, adaptive non-anticipating but action-independent process, and action-dependent market response. For each, define what comparator rewards mean and whether the truncated reward vector is fixed enough for full-information delayed experts. Try to construct a simple action-dependent example where the current upper proof invalidly uses unplayed counterfactual rewards.","title":"Quantifier and causality audit","why_promising":"The upper bound and any necessity statement depend on this modeling choice; resolving it prevents proving theorems for the wrong protocol."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Meta-learning over H may introduce extra delays and lose rates; lower bounds may show adaptation costs are unavoidable.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","rate optimization"],"summary":"Design a horizon-aware but tail-profile-free algorithm that competes over a grid of truncation horizons H and quote grids K, using doubling or a master over delayed learners. Target regret close to inf_H,K [sqrt(T H log K)+T/K+delta_T(H)] plus polylog overhead, under exogenous prices.","title":"Adaptive truncation without tail knowledge","why_promising":"A characterization is more useful if the algorithm does not need to know beta, Delta, or delta in advance; it also tests whether the upper-bound condition is operational."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
